68756837.markup:
(00:00:00) Microsoft 365 Security Alert (00:00:06) The Weakness in MFA (00:00:52) Case File 1: Teams Phishing Inside the Perimeter (00:02:02) Corrective Doctrine for Teams Security (00:06:53) Case File 2: Device Code Flow MFA Evasion (00:08:26) Strengthening Device Code Security (00:13:37) Case File 3: App Consent Abuse (00:15:27) Governance of App Permissions (00:21:03) Case File 4: SharePoint Link Abuse (00:28:06) Token Theft and Session Replay In {{link|https://www.m365.fm/why-m365-security-fails-social-engineering/|this episode}} of M365.fm, Mirko Peters dissects how modern social engineering walks straight through your “secure” Microsoft 365 setup — using Teams, device codes, and OAuth consent — and shows how to redesign policies, detections, and user protocol so pretexting fails on impact. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHAT YOU WILL LEARN
- How attackers weaponize Teams external federation to impersonate IT and harvest MFA approvals{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why device code flows and “helpful” verification messages bypass everything your users think they know about phishing{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How consent phishing and ungoverned app registrations quietly turn “Sign in with Microsoft” into data exfiltration{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why your current {{link|https://www.m365.fm/conditional-access-simply-explained/|Conditional Access}}, Safe Links, and risk policies don’t see the full pretext chain{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to redesign external access, MFA, and Teams policies so chat cannot be used as an elevation vector{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to build concrete KQL detections that correlate external DMs, MFA spikes, device code usage, and mailbox/file activity{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to teach users verification rituals that work under stress instead of vague “be careful” advice{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT Most Microsoft 365 security programs still think in malware, bad URLs, and brute force. Today’s attackers don’t argue with your controls — they use your own channels, branding, and MFA prompts against you.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Teams, device code, and consent flows are all legitimate; the difference between normal and hostile is ceremony: who can contact whom, which flows are allowed, how risk and identity policies respond, and what users are trained to do in the moment. This episode argues that social engineering defense in M365 is not a “user awareness” problem but a systems design problem — and that you can design friction that kills pretext attacks before users have to be perfect. WHY YOUR M365 SECURITY FAILS AGAINST SOCIAL ENGINEERING
- Teams external access is “on by habit,” so any tenant can DM any user with an “IT Support” avatar{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- MFA fatigue is possible because there is no hard rule that “support never asks you to approve a prompt”{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Device code flows are allowed everywhere, with no dedicated policies, detections, or user guidance{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- OAuth consent is under‑governed: users and even admins can grant high‑risk permissions to unverified apps{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Identity risk, collaboration channels, and data activity are monitored separately, so the attack chain never appears as one incident{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

WHAT YOU’LL TAKE AWAY IN PRACTICE
- Concrete Teams external federation and Safe Links settings that cut off unsolicited pretext DMs{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Conditional Access designs that treat Teams and device code flows as elevation vectors, not “just apps”{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Detection patterns that correlate chat, MFA bursts, deviceAuth endpoints, and mailbox/SharePoint changes{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- A verification ritual (phrases, call‑back channels, “never read codes in chat”) that users can actually follow under pressure{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Governance patterns for verified publishers, app consent, and named locations that shrink the social engineering surface{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR This episode is essential for Microsoft 365 security engineers, identity architects, SOC analysts, and IT leaders responsible for user protection in cloud collaboration.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} If you’ve already rolled out MFA, Conditional Access, and Defender, but still worry that one good pretext in Teams or one device code prompt could undo it all, this conversation will give you an end‑to‑end blueprint to fix that.A BOUT THE HOST Mirko Peters is a Microsoft 365 consultant and digital workplace architect focused on building social‑engineering‑resistant security architectures on the Microsoft cloud.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Through M365.fm, Mirko shares real incident patterns, governance models, and detection strategies that help organizations close the gap between “{{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/security-talk/|Zero Trust}} on slides” and how attacks actually unfold in Microsoft 365.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756837/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Become a supporter of this podcast: {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support}}.

68756252.markup:
(00:00:00) The Importance of Secure Microsoft Teams Configuration (00:00:43) Case Studies: Guest Access Gone Wrong (00:02:49) The Truth About Private Channels (00:03:44) MFA for Everyone: The First Layer of Defense (00:05:27) Device Compliance and Session Controls (00:07:14) Guest Access Governance: The Second Layer (00:08:54) DLP: The Tripwires in the Carpet (00:14:09) Guest Life Cycle Management: The Third Layer (00:19:46) Audit and Forensics: The Fourth Layer In {{link|https://www.m365.fm/teams-security-hardening-best-practices/|this episode}} of M365.fm, Mirko Peters shows why Microsoft Teams channels are not secure by default — especially in hybrid, guest‑heavy environments — and walks you through a five‑layer hardening plan you can copy into your own tenant. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHAT YOU WILL LEARN
- How “set and forget” Teams defaults quietly expose data through guests, private channels, and synced libraries{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Two real‑world style incidents: the guest that never left, and the PII paste that turned into a data fork across systems{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why Teams is just the lobby and the real vault lives in {{link|https://www.m365.fm/conditional-access-simply-explained/|Conditional Access}}, {{link|https://www.m365.fm/microsoft-purview-simply-explained/|Purview}} DLP, Entra ID governance, and SharePoint sharing policies{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- A Conditional Access baseline that actually bites: MFA everywhere, no legacy auth, compliant/protected devices for Teams/SharePoint/Exchange, and risk‑aware session controls{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to wire Purview DLP into Teams chat and channels with policy tips, block/override, and tuned confidence levels{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to govern guests with expirations, access reviews, and external sharing controls — especially for private‑channel SharePoint sites{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to prove everything in logs, legal holds, and audits, so your {{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/security-talk/|security}} story survives scrutiny{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT Teams itself is not the security boundary; it is the front door. Real protection comes from identity, devices, data loss prevention, guest governance, and logging that sit underneath the app.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} When those layers are weak or misaligned, one stale guest, one synced private channel, or one tired PII paste can create an incident that Teams alone cannot stop or even fully show you. This episode argues that serious Teams security is not about “locking down chat,” but about designing a layered system where Conditional Access, Purview, Entra ID, and SharePoint all agree on who can see what, from where, and for how long. WHY YOUR TEAMS CHANNELS ARE NOT SECURE BY DEFAULT
- Guests don’t expire, private channels create separate SharePoint sites, and sync clients keep pulling fresh files long after projects end{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Purview DLP is often missing for Teams, so sensitive data pasted into chat silently replicates into email, exports, and local drives{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Conditional Access is set to “good enough,” leaving legacy auth, unmanaged devices, and long‑lived sessions in play{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Guest governance and external sharing policies are loose, and owners assume “project over” means “access over” when it doesn’t{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE FIVE-LAYER HARDENING PLAN YOU’LL HEAR
- Conditional Access that actually bites: MFA for everyone (including guests), legacy auth killed, compliant/protected devices required for Teams/SharePoint/Exchange, and risk‑based session controls{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Purview DLP for Teams chat and channels with high‑confidence block/override rules and mirrored policies for SharePoint and OneDrive{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Entra ID guest governance: expirations, access reviews, limited external collaboration, and special care for private‑channel sites{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- SharePoint sharing and sync controls that reduce blast radius when sync clients and “anyone” links go wrong{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Logging, holds, and audits designed up‑front so you can reconstruct what happened and prove containment{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR This episode is essential for Microsoft 365 security engineers, Teams admins, collaboration platform owners, and cloud architects who run hybrid or partner‑heavy environments.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} If your Teams rollout “just works” but you can’t clearly explain how guests are governed, how DLP reacts in chat, or what happens when a private channel syncs to a contractor’s laptop, this episode will give you a concrete blueprint to fix it. ABOUT THE HOST Mirko Peters is a Microsoft 365 consultant and digital workplace architect focused on building secure, guest‑ready collaboration environments on the Microsoft cloud.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68756252/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Through M365.fm, Mirko shares practical incident stories, policy patterns, and governance models that help organizations turn Teams from a default‑open chat app into a hardened collaboration platform. Become a supporter of this podcast: {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support}}.

68755969.markup:
(00:00:00) The Siloed Security Dilemma (00:00:04) The Rube Goldberg Machine of Security Tools (00:00:18) The Four Blind Spots of Siloed Security (00:01:09) The Limitations of Siloed Tools (00:02:22) The Cost of Inaction (00:04:45) Introducing Defender XDR (00:06:19) Blind Spot 1: 365, Email, and Identity (00:10:36) Blind Spot 2: Identities Without Context (00:14:58) Blind Spot 3: Endpoints Without SaaS and Identity (00:19:01) Blind Spot 4: Cloud Apps Without Integration In {{link|https://www.m365.fm/defender-xdr-essential-for-hybrid-security/|this episode}} of M365.fm, Mirko Peters explains why your current “hybrid {{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/security-talk/|security}}” stack is really just four siloed tools with a shared spreadsheet — and how {{link|https://www.m365.fm/microsoft-defender-xdr-simply-explained/|Defender XDR}} fuses Microsoft 365, Entra ID, endpoints, and cloud apps into one incident graph with one response plan. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHAT YOU WILL LEARN
- Why separate email, identity, endpoint, and cloud app tools create context debt and dwell time instead of security{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How typical hybrid environments (on‑prem AD + Entra ID + roaming devices + SaaS) break classic SOC workflows{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How Defender XDR turns separate alerts (phish, risky sign‑ins, PowerShell abuse, OAuth consent) into a single cross‑domain incident{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How auto‑response can isolate devices, revoke tokens and sessions, roll back mailbox rules, and kill malicious OAuth grants from one place{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why identity, tokens, and consent are the real root causes behind “phantom reinfections”{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to move from four tickets and four consoles to one timeline that shows what actually happened, in what order, and where to respond first{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT Hybrid security isn’t “more vendors + more dashboards”; it is one attack surface pretending to be four. When each domain (email, identity, endpoint, cloud apps) runs its own incident process, your SOC becomes the missing correlation engine — and attackers live in the gaps.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Defender XDR changes the physics by building an incident graph that stitches mailbox rules, consent grants, token issuance, endpoint process chains, and cloud sessions to the same user and device. This episode argues that Defender XDR is not an add‑on; it is the minimum requirement for hybrid environments that want fewer incidents, shorter dwell time, and less manual correlation tax. WHY DEFENDER XDR IS MANDATORY FOR HYBRID
- Microsoft 365 telemetry (phish, Safe Links, mailbox rules, Teams shares) stops living in an email silo and becomes part of one incident{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Entra ID risky sign‑ins and token events are joined with device health, OAuth consent, and SharePoint activity{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Endpoint alerts include the “how we got here” story: phish → consent → token → process chain → exfiltration{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- {{link|https://www.m365.fm/microsoft-defender-for-cloud-apps-simply-explained/|Defender for Cloud Apps}} signals (risky OAuth apps, unusual downloads, shadow IT) are tied directly into the same incident graph{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Auto‑IR can revoke sessions, kill grants, isolate devices, and undo malicious mailbox rules from a single orchestrated playbook{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

KEY TAKEAWAYS
- Siloed tools create context debt that your SOC pays for in dwell time, overtime, and missed intrusions{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- The right question is no longer “what fired?” but “what happened, to whom, across which domains, in what order?”{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Defender XDR lets the platform do the stitching so humans can focus on decisions, not copy‑pasting alert IDs{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Real savings from XDR show up as fewer reinfections, fewer parallel incidents per attacker, and fewer tools your analysts must juggle{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR This episode is essential for security architects, SOC leaders, incident responders, and Microsoft 365 / Azure platform owners responsible for hybrid identity and security.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} If you are still correlating email, identity, endpoint, and cloud‑app alerts in your head or in spreadsheets, this conversation will show you why Defender XDR is now the baseline—not a “nice to have”—for hybrid security. ABOUT THE HOST Mirko Peters is a Microsoft 365 consultant and digital workplace architect focused on building attack‑aware, XDR‑driven security architectures on the Microsoft cloud.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755969/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Through M365.fm, Mirko shares practical incident stories, correlation patterns, and operating models that help security teams turn Defender XDR into a savings engine instead of just another license line.

Become a supporter of this podcast: {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support}}.

68755851.markup:
(00:00:00) Mission Briefing: Protecting Against Tenant Breaches (00:00:41) The Enemy's Tactics: Consent Phishing and Token Theft (00:04:35) The Attack Chain: From Consent to Token Abuse (00:06:22) Detecting and Preventing Consent Phishing (00:14:41) Lateral Movement: From Mailbox to SharePoint (00:17:23) Exfiltration and Data Theft (00:20:26) Implementing Effective Defenses (00:26:01) Closing Remarks and Key Takeaways In {{link|https://www.m365.fm/microsoft-365-attack-chain-explained/|this episode}} of M365.fm, Mirko Peters walks through a real‑world style Microsoft 365 breach where attackers combine consent phishing, AiTM token theft, and OAuth abuse to bypass MFA, replay stolen cookies, and quietly live off the land with Microsoft Graph. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHAT YOU WILL LEARN
- Why perimeter defense and “just add MFA” are lies in modern Microsoft 365 attacks{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How consent phishing, AiTM kits, and multi‑tenant OAuth apps work together to hijack identity and sessions{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Which Entra ID audit and sign‑in events actually matter: “Consent to application”, “ServicePrincipal created”, “AppRoleAssignedTo”, and risky sign‑ins with “requirements satisfied” via cookies{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How attackers use offline_access, refresh tokens, mailbox rules, and scope creep for long‑term persistence{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How Graph, Exchange, and SharePoint telemetry expose mailbox hijack, SharePoint theft, and OAuth‑based exfiltration{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Concrete {{link|https://www.m365.fm/microsoft-sentinel-simply-explained/|Sentinel}}/KQL detection ideas for malicious app consent, token replay, mailbox rule abuse, and Graph exfiltration{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- The one policy family that breaks this entire attack chain: consent control and token protection{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT Most Microsoft 365 breach models still obsess over passwords, URLs, and endpoints. Modern attackers don’t fight your MFA; they reuse your sessions and register their own apps.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} The real M365 attack chain is not “phish → malware → lateral movement”, but “consent → token → Graph”: steal a cookie, gain app consent, escalate scopes, and drain data under the cover of normal cloud traffic. This episode argues that if you’re not governing consent, protecting tokens, and watching service principals, you don’t have a modern M365 defense — you have a firewall nostalgia project. WHY YOUR CURRENT M365 ATTACK MODEL IS WRONG
- It assumes the front door is the login page, not the consent screen and device code flows{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- It treats OAuth apps and service principals as background plumbing, not as first‑class actors in attacks{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- It focuses on password theft, not on session replay, refresh tokens, and offline_access scopes{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- It ignores that most of the critical telemetry already exists in Entra ID, Exchange, SharePoint, and Graph — just without tuned detections{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

WHAT YOU’LL TAKE AWAY IN PRACTICE
- A step‑by‑step picture of the M365 attack chain: from AiTM phish to malicious app consent to Graph‑driven exfiltration{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Concrete Entra and Exchange events to hunt for, plus example Sentinel/KQL patterns to operationalize them{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- A consent hardening plan: disabling broad user consent, enforcing admin workflows, and using verified publishers and low‑risk scopes{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Token and session defenses: Token Protection, risk‑based {{link|https://www.m365.fm/conditional-access-simply-explained/|Conditional Access}}, and revocation practices that make stolen cookies worthless{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR This episode is essential for Microsoft 365 {{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/security-talk/|security}} engineers, identity architects, SOC analysts, and cloud security leaders who own Entra ID, Exchange Online, SharePoint, and Sentinel.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} If your threat model still starts with “user clicks malicious link” and ends with “EDR catches malware,” this conversation will give you a new, identity‑ and consent‑centric view of how M365 actually gets breached. ABOUT THE HOST Mirko Peters is a Microsoft 365 consultant and digital workplace architect focused on building identity‑first, attack‑aware security architectures on the Microsoft cloud.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755851/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Through M365.fm, Mirko shares real‑world breach patterns, KQL approaches, and governance models that help security teams move from perimeter stories to the true Microsoft 365 attack chain. Become a supporter of this podcast: {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support}}.

68755705.markup:
(00:00:00) The MFA Illusion (00:00:00) Consent Bypassing MFA (00:00:54) The Power of OAuth Consent (00:02:08) Persistence and Refresh Tokens (00:02:27) Admin Consent: The Ultimate Key (00:05:47) The Three Non-Negotiable Controls (00:12:11) Case Study: MFA Fails to Stop OAuth Attacks (00:16:48) Detection and Remediation Strategies (00:25:06) Hardening and Ongoing Monitoring (00:28:37) The Consent Control Key Takeaway In this episode of M365.fm, Mirko Peters explains why your MFA and password reset playbooks do nothing against illicit OAuth consent attacks in Entra ID — and shows how attackers use refresh tokens and offline_access to stay in your tenant long after you “kick them out.” {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHAT YOU WILL LEARN
- What illicit OAuth consent grants actually are and why this is authorization abuse, not credential theft{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How a friendly Microsoft consent screen hides powerful scopes like Mail.ReadWrite, Files.ReadWrite.All, and Directory.ReadWrite.All{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why offline_access and refresh tokens keep attackers in your tenant even after password resets, forced sign‑outs, and MFA enforcement{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- The three non‑negotiable Entra controls that collapse most of this attack surface: user consent lockdown, verified publishers, and admin consent workflow{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to detect, prove, and remediate malicious OAuth grants using Entra audit logs, service principals, and Graph / PowerShell queries{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- A step‑by‑step case study that proves why your current “reset + revoke sessions” incident response is not enough{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT Most Microsoft 365 incident playbooks still assume “user account compromised” means “change password, reset sessions, enforce MFA.” In an OAuth consent attack, the attacker doesn’t need your password again — they already have a standing grant with offline_access and Graph scopes that survive all of that.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} The real control point is not the login; it’s the consent event that creates an OAuth2PermissionGrant and a service principal with delegated or application permissions to your data. This episode argues that defending Entra ID means treating app consent, service principals, and scopes as first‑class security objects — and designing your policies, detections, and incident response around them. KEY TOPICS COVERED
- Illicit consent grants 101: delegated vs application permissions, offline_access, and why MFA never fires{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why refresh tokens and OAuth grants outlive password resets and “force sign‑out” actions{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- The three critical Entra configurations: lock down user consent, require verified publishers, and enforce admin consent workflow with least‑privilege scopes{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- High‑signal audit events to hunt: Add servicePrincipalOAuth2PermissionGrant, Add passwordCredential, Add keyCredential, Update application{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to inventory risky apps and grants (offline_access + * .All scopes, tenant‑wide consents, privileged users){{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- A practical remediation and hardening playbook: purge bad grants, rotate secrets, delete rogue service principals, and build a recurring consent hygiene routine{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR This episode is essential for CISOs, identity and access management teams, SOC and detection engineers, and cloud security/platform teams running Microsoft 365 and Entra ID.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} If your organization still treats MFA as the final line of defense and assumes password resets “fix” account‑based attacks, this conversation is your wake‑up call on OAuth, consent, and refresh‑token‑based persistence. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}ABOUT THE HOST Mirko Peters is a Microsoft 365 consultant and digital workplace architect focused on building identity‑first, token‑aware security architectures on the Microsoft cloud.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68755705/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Through M365.fm, Mirko shares practical attack walkthroughs, Entra governance patterns, and real‑world detection and hardening strategies that help security teams close the OAuth consent gap before it becomes their next breach report Explore more on M365.FM
