68127256.markup:
{{link|https://www.m365.fm/ai-agents-simply-explained/|AI agents}} vs. automation, Observe‑Plan‑Act (OPA) loop, governance, guardrails and reliability – {{link|https://www.m365.fm/agent-vs-automation-why-most-get-it-wrong/|this episode}} is for people searching “what is an AI agent?”, “agent vs automation difference”, “OPA loop explained”, or “how to govern AI {{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/copilot-talk/|agents}} at work” and wanting a clear, practical breakdown rather than marketing buzzwords. We start with where most confusion begins: you press a button, something runs, and it looks smart – but under the hood, most so‑called “agents” are just scripts wearing a new label, and that has real consequences for risk and expectations in your organization. From there, we unpack the illusion of automation: why highly polished scripts can feel intelligent, where they break the moment context shifts, and why that brittleness matters if you’re about to hand critical workflows to “bots”. You’ll hear why automation is more like a vending machine – reliable, repeatable, zero awareness – while genuine agents behave more like a junior teammate who watches what’s happening, remembers what worked last time, and adjusts on the fly when reality doesn’t match the script. Then we dive into the Observe‑Plan‑Act engine as the real heart of an agent, not a marketing label. We walk through how agents observe systems and signals, plan their own next steps, act through your existing tools and APIs, and loop through feedback instead of just replaying a macro – plus what this means for error‑handling, safety and trust when things go wrong. Finally, we map the five “organs” of an agent body – perception, memory, reasoning and more – so you can see what must be in place before you put an agent anywhere near real‑world workflows, customers or data. WHAT YOU WILL LEARN
- Why most “agents” in marketing are actually just glorified automation scripts.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How automation works, where it excels, and where it fails the moment context changes.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- The core difference between a vending‑machine workflow and an agent‑like teammate.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How the Observe‑Plan‑Act loop makes agents adaptive instead of purely mechanical.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- The five core “organs” every serious agent system needs to be reliable.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why mislabeling automation as agency leads to broken trust and governance problems.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- When you should deliberately choose simple automation instead of agents.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Practical implications for safety, guardrails and decision‑rights in agent design.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT The core insight of this episode is that calling everything an “agent” doesn’t make your systems smarter – it only blurs the line between safe, predictable automation and genuinely autonomous behavior. Once you understand the Observe‑Plan‑Act loop and the five organs of an agent body, you can design systems that either remain clean automation on purpose, or step into true agency with the governance, guardrails and expectations that level of autonomy demands. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHO THIS IS FOR
- Engineering and platform teams exploring AI agents in production workflows.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Product leaders and founders trying to decide when to build agents vs. automation.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Architects and governance leads defining policies for AI systems and autonomy.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Data and AI practitioners who want a clear mental model of OPA‑based agents.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Leaders burned by “intelligent bots” hype who want realistic expectations and language.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68127256/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

ABOUT THE HOST Mirko Peters is a Microsoft 365 consultant and host of M365.FM, where he explores modern work, AI and productivity with a focus on how systems behave in real organizations, not just demos. He helps teams translate concepts like agents, automation, governance and guardrails into practical designs that are understandable to both IT and business stakeholders. Become a supporter of this podcast: {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support}}.

68120572.markup:
{{link|https://www.m365.fm/azure-ai-foundry-simply-explained/|Azure AI Foundry}} {{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/copilot-talk/|agents}}, Threads, Runs, Run Steps, observability and governance – {{link|https://www.m365.fm/your-azure-ai-foundrys-agent-army-why-it-wins/|this episode}} is for people searching “Azure AI Foundry agents explained”, “Threads Runs Run Steps logging”, “enterprise AI observability” or “how to govern {{link|https://www.m365.fm/ai-agents-simply-explained/|AI agents}} in Azure” and wanting a concrete mental model instead of marketing slides. We start with the part almost nobody tells you: when you deploy an AI in Azure AI Foundry, you’re not just spinning up one big model – you’re dropping it into a managed runtime where every message, tool call and run step is logged and traced, turning experiments into something auditable and enterprise‑ready.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} From there, we zoom in on the squad leader: the Azure AI agent built from three core gears – Model, Instructions and Tools. You’ll hear why this triad matters so much for reproducibility and control, how “just a chat endpoint” is like sending a captain into the field without orders or gear, and how Foundry lets you mix and match models (GPT‑4o, leaner models, or even others) while keeping a stable, governed mission frame around them. We use battlefield and gaming analogies to make the architecture intuitive: the model as the brain, instructions as mission orders, and tools as specialized equipment wired into your enterprise systems and APIs.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Then we move to Threads – your battlefront log. Unlike disposable chat windows, Threads are persistent conversation sessions that store structured messages, including text, images, files and generated code, with roles and timestamps. You’ll see why that gives you both continuity for the agent and a durable ledger for compliance, debugging and audits, and how it compares to systems where conversations vanish and you’re left with screenshots and guesswork when regulators or stakeholders ask “what exactly happened here?”.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Then we move to Threads – your battlefront log. Unlike disposable chat windows, Threads are persistent conversation sessions that store structured messages, including text, images, files and generated code, with roles and timestamps. You’ll see why that gives you both continuity for the agent and a durable ledger for compliance, debugging and audits, and how it compares to systems where conversations vanish and you’re left with screenshots and guesswork when regulators or stakeholders ask “what exactly happened here?”.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}Finally, we dissect Runs and Run Steps – where missions actually fire and where observability becomes real. Runs take the context in a Thread and execute it through the agent, with explicit statuses and a full breakdown of each action. Run Steps give you chess‑notation style traceability over the execution path: which tools were called, what code ran, what messages were produced and in which order, so you can debug failures, prove compliance and build trust in how your “agent army” behaves across tools, data and teams. By the end, you’ll see how Azure AI Foundry turns AI from a loose cannon into a disciplined, logged and governable digital squad you can actually put in front of real workflows. WHAT YOU WILL LEARN
- How Azure AI Foundry’s managed runtime logs messages, tool calls and run steps for auditability.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why real Azure AI agents are built from Model, Instructions and Tools, not just a prompt.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to think about your main agent as a “squad leader” coordinating tools and data sources.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- What Threads are, how they persist conversations and why they matter for compliance.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How Runs kick off execution and how Run statuses help monitor live workloads.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why Run Steps are essential for observability, debugging and structured traceability.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- The risks of running “just chat” without logs, and how Foundry closes that gap.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Practical implications for governance, guardrails and enterprise AI readiness in Azure.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT The core insight of this episode is that the power of Azure AI Foundry isn’t just the model catalog – it’s the way agents, Threads, Runs and Run Steps combine into a disciplined, observable system you can actually govern. When you treat your Azure AI agent as a structured squad leader with clear orders, tools and a full mission log, you move from ad‑hoc text generation to a tracked, auditable “agent army” that enterprises can trust in real workflows. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHO THIS IS FOR
- Platform and engineering teams building on Azure AI Foundry.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Architects and governance leads defining observability and logging for AI systems.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Product and AI leaders designing multi‑tool agents for real business workflows.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/68120572/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
