67419834.markup:
What Is {{link|https://www.m365.fm/microsoft-intune-simply-explained/|Microsoft Intune}} Used For? Most people still think Intune is just “mobile device management,” but in modern Microsoft 365 it’s really an access and protection layer that connects identity, devices, apps and data. In {{link|https://www.m365.fm/what-is-microsoft-intune-used-for/|this episode}}, you’ll hear how Intune, Azure AD and Defender work together so you’re not just pushing policies to laptops—you’re shaping conditional, identity‑based access to corporate resources across work and personal devices. We walk from the old GPO world into an identity‑first model: {{link|https://www.m365.fm/conditional-access-simply-explained/|Conditional Access}}, compliant devices, app protection policies and scenarios like contractors, BYOD and remote work that show why Intune has become a strategic part of your {{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/security-talk/|security}} and compliance story, not just an IT tool. We start with the old world of classic device management and GPOs. On-premises, you mainly cared about domain-joined Windows devices on your network, and security meant “is the laptop configured correctly.” Today, users work from anywhere, on many different devices, across SaaS apps you don’t fully control. You’ll hear how Intune shifts the focus from managing just Windows machines to managing the combination of user, device posture and app, so security travels with the identity instead of being bound to a single network. Then we zoom into what Intune actually does for you day-to-day. We cover core capabilities like device enrollment and compliance policies, app deployment and updates, configuration profiles, and security baselines that give you consistent, repeatable settings across your fleet. You’ll learn how Conditional Access uses Intune’s compliance signals to decide who gets in and who doesn’t, and how app protection policies help you protect corporate data even on personal devices—without fully managing the entire phone or laptop. Finally, we tie Intune to business and compliance outcomes. It’s not just about “locking things down,” but about showing that only healthy, compliant devices access sensitive resources, that lost devices can be wiped, and that data stays within managed apps. By the end of the episode, you’ll be able to explain Intune in one sentence (“it makes sure only trusted users and devices can work with company data”) and know which features to focus on first for your own environment WHAT YOU’LL LEARN{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why Intune is more than classic mobile device management in the Microsoft 365 era.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How Intune, Entra ID (Azure AD) and Microsoft Defender work together to secure access.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- The basics of device enrollment, compliance policies, app deployment and app protection.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How Intune supports scenarios like BYOD, contractors and remote work while keeping data safe.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}The core insight of this episode is that Intune is no longer “just an MDM tool”—it’s a key part of your identity-based security model. Once you see Intune as the policy and compliance engine behind Conditional Access, you stop thinking about it as a way to lock down devices and start using it to intelligently decide which combinations of user, device and app you actually trust with your company’s data. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- IT admins and architects who are new to Intune or only using a small part of its capabilities.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Security and compliance teams who need clearer guardrails for access from laptops, mobiles and BYOD devices.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Business and IT leaders who want a simple, non-technical way to understand where Intune fits in their Microsoft 365 security story.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

ABOUT THE AUTHOR / HOST {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}Mirko Peters is a Microsoft 365 and endpoint management consultant and host of the M365.FM podcast, helping organizations move from legacy device management to modern, identity-based protection with Intune. He works with IT and security teams to design enrollment, compliance and app protection strategies so users can work from anywhere while company data stays under control.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67419834/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Become a supporter of this podcast: {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support}}.

67411474.markup:
Step-by-Step Guide to Automating GRC Reports with {{link|https://www.m365.fm/power-automate-simply-explained/|Power Automate}} Manually building GRC reports in spreadsheets doesn’t just waste time—it quietly adds error risk and compliance gaps into the very process that’s supposed to prevent them. In {{link|https://www.m365.fm/step-by-step-guide-to-automating-grc-reports-with-power-automate/|this episode}}, I show how you can use Power Automate to connect your actual systems of record—SharePoint, Excel, Dataverse, ticketing tools—and turn scattered evidence, risk registers and incident logs into a repeatable reporting pipeline that runs on schedule and produces consistent, auditable output. Instead of end-of-month stress and copy‑paste marathons, you get near‑real‑time GRC reports that reflect today’s reality, not last quarter’s snapshot. We start with why manual GRC reporting is a bigger risk than it looks on the surface. Long evidence-collection cycles, endless spreadsheet reconciliations and email ping‑pong create lag, inconsistency and hidden errors that only show up under audit pressure. You’ll hear how hybrid work and tool sprawl turned GRC into a data scavenger hunt across SharePoint folders, Excel files, service desk tools and dashboards—making it almost impossible to keep reports current without automation. Then we break down what really goes into a GRC report and how to model that in {{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/power-platform-talk/|Microsoft 365}} and {{link|https://www.m365.fm/power-platform-simply-explained/|Power Platform}}. Control evidence, risk registers, incident logs and metrics all live in different systems, so we map where they should live (for example, evidence in SharePoint libraries, risks in lists or Dataverse, incidents in a ticketing or case system) and how Power Automate can orchestrate them. You’ll learn how to build flows that listen to changes, normalize data into consistent structures, and assemble report-ready datasets without manual stitching. Finally, we turn the pipeline into actual, repeatable reports. We walk through using scheduled flows to pull fresh data, aggregate key metrics, and output standardized GRC reports—whether that’s Excel, PDF, or feeding Power BI dashboards—so leadership and auditors see the same, up‑to‑date story every time. By the end, you’ll have a pattern you can copy: from “hand‑built, high‑risk GRC reports” to an automated reporting system that is faster, more reliable and easier to audit. WHAT YOU’LL LEARN{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why manual, spreadsheet‑based GRC reporting quietly increases compliance risk.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- What really goes into a GRC report: evidence, risk registers, incidents and metrics across multiple systems.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to use Power Automate to connect SharePoint, Excel, Dataverse and ticketing tools into a reporting pipeline.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to generate consistent, auditable GRC reports on a schedule instead of scrambling at the end of the month or quarter.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}The core insight of this episode is that GRC reports aren’t “documents”—they are the last step of a data flow. Once you treat your evidence, risks and incidents as connected data sources and let Power Automate orchestrate them, reporting stops being a manual, error‑prone project and becomes a predictable, automated outcome of how you already work. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Compliance and risk teams stuck in spreadsheet‑driven GRC reporting cycles.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Power Platform and Microsoft 365 teams asked to “automate GRC reports” without a clear blueprint.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Leaders who need timely, trustworthy compliance insights instead of outdated snapshots.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

ABOUT THE AUTHOR / HOST {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67411474/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}Mirko Peters is a Microsoft 365 and Power Platform consultant and host of the M365.FM podcast, helping organizations turn manual, spreadsheet‑heavy compliance work into automated, auditable workflows in Power Automate. He works with GRC, IT and business teams to design reporting pipelines that pull from real systems of record, so governance reports arrive on time, with less effort and far fewer surprises. Become a supporter of this podcast: {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support}}.

67405208.markup:
Dynamics 365 {{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/copilot-talk/|Copilot}} for Field Service: From Manual Notes to Real-Time, AI-Powered Work Orders If your field technicians still spend 20 minutes per job typing notes, you’re not just losing time—you’re losing data quality. In {{link|https://www.m365.fm/dynamics-365-copilot-for-field-service/|this episode}}, we look at how Dynamics 365 Copilot can turn spoken updates and on-site context into structured work orders, parts usage and service history entries automatically. Instead of end-of-day paperwork marathons and guesswork from scribbled notes, you get accurate, real-time documentation that flows straight into scheduling, inventory, billing and customer communication.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} We start with the hidden paperwork bottleneck most service managers never see. The real delay isn’t the repair itself—it’s the admin drag that follows every completed job: handwritten notes, re-typing into multiple systems, and trying to remember details hours later. You’ll hear how this lag quietly breaks schedules, slows invoicing, and injects errors into everything from inventory counts to warranty claims, even in teams that look efficient from the outside. Then we explore what changes when work orders start to “write themselves.” Copilot captures a short voice note right after the job, understands which asset, parts and issue it’s about, and maps that into structured fields plus a clean narrative service history. Because it knows the customer, the equipment and the existing records, it can normalise terminology, reduce error rates and give dispatchers and back-office teams complete, consistent data without chasing technicians for missing details. Finally, we connect this to real-time support and decision-making. Once the system understands what happened on-site, it can surface next-best actions, maintenance patterns, and recurring issues across your installed base. By the end of the episode, you’ll see how Dynamics 365 Copilot doesn’t just save minutes in the van—it closes the gap between “job done” and “job documented,” so technicians, planners and customers all operate from the same, up-to-date picture. WHAT YOU’LL LEARN{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why manual post-job documentation quietly breaks schedules, invoicing and reporting.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How Dynamics 365 Copilot turns spoken technician updates into structured, system-ready work orders.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How cleaner, real-time data improves scheduling, inventory, warranty handling and customer communication.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}The core insight of this episode is that field service isn’t just about fixing things—it’s about capturing what happened in a way your systems can trust. Once you let Copilot handle the documentation layer, your technicians stop being part-time data-entry clerks and your organisation finally gets the accurate, timely field data it needs to run efficiently. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Field service leaders who want to reduce admin load without losing detail.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Operations and service managers struggling with late, incomplete or inconsistent work orders.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Dynamics 365 and {{link|https://www.m365.fm/power-platform-simply-explained/|Power Platform}} teams looking to bring AI assistance into real-world field workflows.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67405208/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

