67391246.markup:
Fabric Lakehouse Governance & Data Lineage Most teams only notice their data lineage when something breaks—a number changes, a column disappears, or a report no longer matches the source. In {{link|https://www.m365.fm/fabric-lakehouse-governance-data-lineage/|this episode}}, we trace the full journey of your data inside {{link|https://www.m365.fm/microsoft-fabric-simply-explained/|Microsoft Fabric}}’s Lakehouse, from ingestion through transformation into analytics, and show how automatic lineage, permission inheritance and audit trails turn that “invisible middle” into a map you can actually govern. We start with why modern analytics environments feel like a renovated building rather than a straight hallway. Data doesn’t flow in one simple pipeline; it weaves through pipelines, Lakehouse tables, semantic models and reports, often across multiple workspaces and teams. You’ll hear how Fabric’s built‑in lineage capture turns that maze into a visual chain: every Data Factory pipeline run, every Lakehouse table, every downstream dataset and report are linked automatically, so you can see exactly where a value changed instead of guessing across tools and exports.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Then we connect lineage to permissions and governance. Fabric uses workspaces as the central control point, so the same permission model applies across Lakehouse, semantic models and {{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/data-talk/|Power BI}} reports instead of fragmenting into separate ACLs at every layer. We unpack how permission inheritance means a locked‑down Lakehouse table stays locked when it feeds a dataset or report, reducing security drift and eliminating the classic gap where someone only meant to share a dashboard but accidentally exposed raw data underneath. Finally, we look at why the audit trail is just as important as access control. With Fabric logging who changed what and when, you can move beyond “check the logs” as a last‑minute panic and treat it as a routine part of operations and compliance. By the end of the episode, you’ll see how lineage, permissions and audit work together: you don’t just know the current state of a dataset, you can reconstruct the full path it took, who touched it along the way, and where to focus your governance rules so they follow the data automatically instead of being maintained by hand. WHAT YOU’LL LEARN{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why data paths in Fabric Lakehouse are more like a maze than a straight pipeline—and how automatic lineage makes that maze visible.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How Fabric’s workspace‑based permission model and inheritance keep Lakehouse, datasets and reports aligned.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How lineage metadata becomes the foundation for governance rules, not just pretty diagrams.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why a unified audit trail across transformations and access changes is critical for troubleshooting and compliance.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}The core insight of this episode is that governance only really works when it travels with the data. Once Microsoft Fabric captures lineage by default, applies permissions consistently across Lakehouse and analytics, and records who changed what and when, you move from guessing about how your data is used to being able to show it—step by step, end to end. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Data and analytics teams running or planning a Fabric Lakehouse who need more than ad‑hoc documentation.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Governance, risk and compliance teams who require traceable data flows and consistent access control.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Architects who want a practical mental model for how lineage, permissions and audit fit together in Fabric.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

ABOUT THE AUTHOR / HOST {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67391246/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}Mirko Peters is a Microsoft 365 and data platform consultant and host of the M365.FM podcast, helping organizations design Fabric Lakehouse environments where lineage, governance and analytics work together instead of fighting each other. He works with data, BI and governance teams to turn invisible pipelines into mapped, governed data products that hold up under real‑world troubleshooting and audit pressure. Become a supporter of this podcast: {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support}}.

67389972.markup:
Governed AI: Keeping {{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/copilot-talk/|Copilot}} Secure and Compliant If you think Copilot only shows users what they already have permission to see, you’re one Graph permission away from a nasty surprise. In {{link|https://www.m365.fm/governed-ai-keeping-copilot-secure-and-compliant/|this episode}}, I walk through how unmanaged Graph and app permissions let Copilot quietly overreach—surfacing sensitive SharePoint files, Teams content and financial data your compliance team never intended to expose—and how to lock things down with a governed, least‑privilege design. We start with what most organizations get wrong about Copilot’s access model. There’s a dangerous assumption that “if I’m signed in as me, Copilot only sees what I see,” but many deployments rely on application‑level Graph permissions that operate far beyond a single user’s rights. You’ll hear concrete examples where a junior user gets board‑level meeting notes or full pipeline numbers, not because their account was misconfigured, but because Copilot’s app registration was granted broad tenant‑wide access early in the rollout and never revisited. Then we look at how to spot these gaps before they turn into incidents. Using Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD) and {{link|https://www.m365.fm/microsoft-purview-simply-explained/|Purview}}’s audit capabilities, we break down how to identify Copilot’s app registrations, understand which Graph permissions are actually in use, and trace AI‑driven access patterns across SharePoint, Teams and other workloads. You’ll learn how to set up targeted audit searches and reviews so “what did Copilot pull, for whom, and from where?” becomes an answerable question—not guesswork after a suspicious answer shows up in someone’s draft.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Finally, we move from visibility to guardrails that actually work. We cover how to redesign permissions around least privilege, where DLP and {{link|https://www.m365.fm/microsoft-purview-information-protection-simply-explained/|Purview information protection}} should step in for AI scenarios, and how to tune policies so they block real risk without breaking everyday productivity. By the end of the episode, you’ll have a practical blueprint to run Copilot as a governed, auditable service: scoped Graph permissions, meaningful DLP and label rules, and monitoring that lets you sleep at night instead of hoping your AI isn’t seeing too much WHAT YOU’LL LEARN{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why Copilot can overreach beyond a user’s normal access if Graph permissions are too broad.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to find and review Copilot app registrations and Graph permissions in Entra ID.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to use Purview audit to see what Copilot is actually accessing across M365.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to design DLP, labeling and least‑privilege permission models specifically for AI scenarios.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}The core insight of this episode is that Copilot’s risk isn’t “AI gone rogue”—it’s ungoverned access. Once you treat Graph permissions, DLP and Purview policies as the real control plane for Copilot, you can keep AI genuinely useful for users while staying inside the security and compliance boundaries your organization actually signed off on. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Security and compliance teams responsible for approving or reviewing Copilot deployments.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Microsoft 365 and Entra ID admins managing Graph permissions and app registrations.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Leaders who want the benefits of Copilot without turning it into an invisible data‑exposure risk.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

ABOUT THE AUTHOR / HOST {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67389972/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}Mirko Peters is a Microsoft 365 and security consultant and host of the M365.FM podcast, helping organizations roll out Copilot and other AI tools with governance, not guesswork. He works with IT, security and compliance teams to design Graph permission models, DLP and Purview configurations, and monitoring that keep AI helpful for users while keeping sensitive data where it belongs. Become a supporter of this podcast: {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss|https://www.spreaker.com/podcast/m365-fm-microsoft-365-azure-cloud-power-platform-ai--6704921/support}}.

67386549.markup:
{{link|https://www.m365.fm/categories/security-talk/|Zero Trust}} by Design in Microsoft 365 & Dynamics 365 If your Microsoft 365 tenant talks to Dynamics 365, Azure and other SaaS tools, your attack surface is bigger than any single product team can see. In {{link|https://www.m365.fm/zero-trust-by-design-in-microsoft-365-dynamics-365/|this episode}}, I show why “Zero Trust = MFA in M365” is a dangerous illusion—and how Zero Trust by design treats M365 and D365 as one interdependent system, so attackers can’t simply bypass your hard work in one platform by walking through a weaker door in another.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} We start with the classic mistake: rolling out strong {{link|https://www.m365.fm/conditional-access-simply-explained/|Conditional Access}} and MFA for Microsoft 365 while Dynamics 365 quietly runs on looser or mismatched rules. You’ll hear how this creates real incidents in the gaps between systems: stolen credentials blocked at SharePoint but still accepted in Dynamics, finance data exposed via bookmarks and tokens, and users who never see a single warning prompt while attackers generate exports and invoices in the background.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Then we look at what changes when your policies share live risk signals across workloads. Azure AD Conditional Access evaluates every sign‑in for risk, while Dynamics 365 role‑based security decides what a user can actually do—but they only become truly effective when they respond to the same risk state in real time. We walk through how to let identity risk, device health and session context flow into D365 decisions, so a risky sign‑in in M365 can automatically restrict sensitive exports or finance actions in Dynamics without you duplicating rules manually.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}} Finally, we zoom out to identity segmentation that doesn’t break everyday work. Zero Trust by design means segmenting users and access based on real risk and business roles across M365 and D365, not handing everyone a “master key” because it’s convenient. By the end, you’ll have a clear mental model and practical starting points for aligning Conditional Access, D365 roles and cross‑system risk signals—so every login, every transaction and every API call across Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365 goes through the same level of scrutiny. WHAT YOU’LL LEARN{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Why focusing Zero Trust on just Microsoft 365 leaves exploitable gaps in Dynamics 365.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How attackers abuse inconsistent Conditional Access and MFA policies across connected systems.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How Azure AD Conditional Access and D365 role-based security can share live risk signals.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- How to think about identity segmentation across M365 and D365 without breaking real workflows.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

THE CORE INSIGHT {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}The core insight of this episode is that Zero Trust only works when every connected service enforces it the same way. Once Microsoft 365, Dynamics 365 and other SaaS tools share identity risk, device health and session context as one coordinated fabric, you close the “side doors” attackers rely on and move from Zero Trust on paper to Zero Trust by design. {{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}WHO THIS EPISODE IS FOR{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Security and IT teams responsible for both Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Architects designing Zero Trust strategies across multiple Microsoft cloud services.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}
- Business and finance leaders who depend on Dynamics 365 and want more than “MFA is on” as an answer.{{link|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266|https://www.spreaker.com/cms/episodes/67386549/edit/info?filter=NETWORK&network=18613266}}

